NAME: flint-s3-csi
LAST DEPLOYED: Wed Sep 30 12:06:33 2026
NAMESPACE: flint-system
STATUS: deployed
REVISION: 1
TEST SUITE: None
NOTES:
flint-s3-csi is installed in namespace flint-system.

Confirm the driver registered on every node (the registrar sidecar does
this; give a fresh node a few seconds):

  kubectl get csidriver s3.csi.chert.us
  kubectl -n flint-system rollout status ds/flint-s3-csi-node
  kubectl -n flint-system rollout status deploy/flint-s3-broker

Declare a mount IN THE TENANT'S NAMESPACE, and say who may use it:

  apiVersion: chert.us/v1alpha1
  kind: FlintPassthroughMount
  metadata: { name: datasets, namespace: team-a }
  spec:
    bucket: my-bucket
    keyPrefix: datasets/imagenet
    endpoint: http://minio.flint-system.svc:9000   # unset ⇒ AWS S3
    readOnly: true
    consumers:
      serviceAccounts: ["trainer"]   # ABSENT = DENY; "*" = any SA here
    identity:
      mode: broker                   # broker | webIdentity | static | ambient

Then a pod mounts it with a volume — no label, no sidecar:

  spec:
    serviceAccountName: trainer      # must be in consumers
    volumes:
      - name: data
        csi:
          driver: s3.csi.chert.us
          readOnly: true
          volumeAttributes:
            chert.us/mount: datasets
            # chert.us/uid: "1001"   # presentation only; default 65534
    containers:
      - name: app
        volumeMounts:
          - { name: data, mountPath: /mnt/s3, readOnly: true }

volumeAttributes are a REQUEST, never an authorization: exactly one of
chert.us/mount / chert.us/workspace, optionally chert.us/uid and
chert.us/gid, and every other key is refused by name. bucket, keyPrefix,
endpoint, image and credentials come from the CR and only the CR.

WHERE THINGS RUN. Each mounted volume is one worker pod in
flint-workers, pinned to the tenant's node:

  kubectl -n flint-workers get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/managed-by=flint-s3-csi-node

The worker is placed on the tenant's node directly (spec.nodeName), so
the scheduler reserves no room for it and it counts against the node's
maxPods like any pod. A node with no room refuses it (a WorkerNotAdmitted
event on the tenant, which stays ContainerCreating and is NOT
rescheduled) until capacity frees. Size nodes for two pods per
S3-mounted tenant, or let read-only consumers of one dataset share a
mounter (spec.sharing.readOnly).

The tenant pod carries no credential and no privilege and is admissible
under PodSecurity `restricted`. The node plugin is the privileged
component (one per node); the broker is the one with a standing
credential. A refused publish shows up as a FailedMount event on the
tenant pod, retried with backoff — `kubectl describe pod` names the
reason (a missing CR, a ServiceAccount not in consumers, a refused
attribute).

IDENTITY. Every publish registers with the broker, and every credential
is minted from the POD's own kubelet-issued token (1 h, audience
s3.csi.chert.us) after a TokenReview. A pod
gets a credential only for a CR whose consumers name its ServiceAccount,
120 s at a time.

THIS INSTALL RUNS THE STATIC BACKEND: the broker hands the ONE key set in
Secret s3-broker-static to every entitled pod. The
pod is still authenticated and authorized per publish, but every project
shares that key at the bucket. Move to broker.backend sts or rest when a
per-project credential source exists.

A MOUNTER CRASH IS STILL NOT RECOVERABLE IN PLACE. Containers that
already opened the mount read ENOTCONN once the worker dies; the volume
stays unhealthy until the pod is recreated. Run S3-mounted workloads
under a controller.

THE MOUNT PRESENTS AS ONE UID. NodePublish never sees the pod's
securityContext, so nothing can default from runAsUser. Ownership is the
volume's chert.us/uid attribute if set, ELSE the CR's spec.uid, else
65534 — the ATTRIBUTE WINS over the CR. gid resolves the same way and
defaults to whatever uid resolved to. With nothing declared, files no
longer present as root as they did under the webhook. A lean workspace
has no default: it refuses to publish without a uid, and refuses uid 0,
because the syncer must run as the app's uid to read what the app wrote.

IDENTITY MODES. broker, webIdentity and static are the driver's own and
run unchanged against S3, MinIO or Ceph RGW. ambient hands the worker
nothing: whatever the SDK's default credential chain finds on the node
(environment, a shared file, a web-identity token, a container
credential URI, instance metadata) is the identity, and when it finds
nothing the FailedMount event carries the chain's own error. The
platform must let a pod complete that chain — on a cloud instance
behind an overlay network, the metadata service's hop limit has to
admit pods — and the driver does not proxy or inject it.

NODE LOSS. A tenant on a node that dies is Kubernetes' to reschedule; a
Deployment's replacement pod mounts on its new node and the dead node's
workers go with the node. What Kubernetes does NOT do without a
cloud-controller-manager is delete the Node object of an instance that
has vanished. Until someone does, a rolling update of this chart's
DaemonSet stalls: the controller picks the dead node's pod first, it can
never finish terminating, and with maxUnavailable 1 no live node is
updated. `kubectl delete node <name>` is the operator's step on such a
cluster; the roll then proceeds. Mounts on live nodes keep serving
throughout — a plugin roll does not unmount tenants.

WHEN A DRAIN COULD NOT BE ATTESTED. If a lean syncer dies, is evicted or
runs out of grace before its final publish completes, the node plugin
does NOT delete the workspace — it moves the tree aside and raises a
DrainNotAttested event on the tenant. What the tenant wrote since its
last publish is still on that node:

  <kubelet-root>/plugins/s3.csi.chert.us/undrained/<volume>-<unix>

By default (workers.quota: false) that directory holds the tree itself,
at <...>/undrained/<volume>-<unix>/tree. With workers.quota: true it holds
an ext4 IMAGE, not a browsable tree, so recovering it means mounting it
read-only:

  mount -o loop,ro <...>/undrained/<volume>-<unix>/tree.img /mnt/recover

Nothing ever removes these, by design — losing a tenant's last writes to
an automatic sweep would be worse than keeping them. The consequence is
that they accumulate until an operator reviews and deletes them, and a
node whose root filesystem fills crosses kubelet's DiskPressure
threshold, at which point kubelet evicts pods that have nothing to do
with the workspace that was preserved. Size the node's root filesystem
for the workspaces it may hold (a preserved plain tree costs what it
contains; with workers.quota: true each costs at least the ext4 metadata
its sizeLimitGib implies — roughly 0.5 GiB for a 20 GiB workspace — plus
every block ever written in it), and sweep the directory
as part of routine operations.

NODE SHUTDOWN, AND WHAT SURVIVES IT. A graceful shutdown drains: the
syncer publishes its outstanding work on SIGTERM, so a reboot or a
planned cloud-instance termination keeps it. A machine that simply
stops — power cut, kernel panic, instance failure — runs no drain at
all, and everything written since the last publish is gone; `floorSecs`
on the workspace is what bounds that window. The ordering between a
tenant and its worker during a shutdown is kubelet's graceful node
shutdown, which is OFF unless `shutdownGracePeriod` is set on the
kubelet; see workers.priorityClassName in values.yaml.
